 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Correlation Term:** The 2D integral of a Gaussian with a non-diagonal covariance matrix contains an intractable correlation term $b(x-\hat{\mu}_x)(y-\hat{\mu}_y)$. Analytic-Splatting resolves this by diagonalizing the covariance matrix and rotating the integration domain to align with the eigenvectors, approximating the 2D integral as a product of two independent 1D integrals [S2].
-*   **Small Standard Deviation:** Unlike Mip-Splatting which convolves with a Gaussian kernel (approximating the integral as $g \circledast g_w$), Analytic-Splatting calculates the integral analytically. This avoids large gaps for high-frequency components (small $\sigma$) where convolution-based methods might fail [S2].
-*   **Precision:** The logistic approximation is differentiable, allowing for gradient computation. However, extreme values of $x/\sigma$ in the exponential function may lead to overflow or underflow; standard numerical stability techniques (e.g., clamping) should be applied [S2].
